Original commit body from 8825dca181acda957932151ee7a70413a63d72a5:

make_symbol_id now applies the ADR-0036 Ruling-1 ':' -> '.' substitution to the
name slot. The canonical parse is anchored from the RIGHT (span, name, kind =
parts[-3:]), so a colon in the name slot shifts every anchor left by one and the
id stops parsing; colons in the path slot are harmless and stay verbatim, which
is why Rust's std::cmp module ids are canonical.

PREMISE REVERSED BY MEASUREMENT. This fold was deferred since 2026-06-13 and a
guard test enforced the deferral, on the stated ground that an always-on rewrite
"would silently churn colon-bearing source identifiers like Obj-C selectors."
That reads as "currently-good ids become churned." Probing the objc analyzer on
one keyword-selector method:

  Symbol.name  FileManager.removeItemAtPath:error:
  before       objc:T.m:2-4:FileManager.removeItemAtPath:error::method  (7 segs)
  after        objc:T.m:2-4:FileManager.removeItemAtPath.error.:method  (5 segs)

Those ids were ALREADY unparseable. Selector-style languages are the largest
beneficiaries of the fold, not its casualties. The guard test is inverted rather
than deleted, and carries the before/after in its docstring so the reversal is
self-documenting.

js_ts.py's private _make_symbol_id was an f-string copy of the grammar -- the
mirror the item predicted -- and now delegates, so it stops opting out of the
minter's guarantees.

The two objc assertions that matched the raw selector as a substring of the id
are rewritten to resolve the edge to its target Symbol and assert on
Symbol.name (the fidelity home). That is stronger than the original: it proves
the edge points at a real node rather than at a string that happens to contain
the selector text.

PART 4 NOT BUILT, ON A MEASURED FALSE PREMISE. The item also specifies "a
sibling validator sub-check that the id name-slot == sanitized(Symbol.name)".
That equality is not an invariant this codebase holds: it fails on 1430 of
40855 canonical ids (3.50%), of which 1398 are the documented make_file_id
sentinel {lang}:{path}:1-1:file:file, and the residual 32 are four further
deliberate conventions -- Rust's '::' -> '.' COLLAPSE (not a per-colon
replace), the js/ts '@NN' occurrence suffix, the synthetic websocket route
normalization, and the Maven coordinate fold. The weaker requirement the
grammar actually states -- name slot colon-free -- is already enforced:
_CANONICAL_ID_PATTERN defines that slot as [^:]*, so a colon-bearing name fails
the shape check and is caught by _check_id_format (which since e32d074d2c
reports it as colon_in_name_slot). _check_id_roundtrip only runs on ids that
already matched, so the sub-check would be unreachable code.

Evidence: 11742 tests pass, 100% coverage on changed sources. Fresh
self-analysis over 40857 symbols holds id_format at 0 and shows all 7
colon-bearing Symbol.names carrying colon-free id slots. Note the self-corpus
does NOT exercise the new behaviour -- those 7 already routed through the
explicit helper -- so the corpus evidence is no-regression; the ObjC probe is
what demonstrates the repair.

Stale gate corrected on the item: the description said this "must ride the v6
stable_id bump", written when v6 was upcoming; STABLE_ID_SCHEME is now v8. No
collision with the WI-talos v9/v10 sequencing either -- the only five sites
that alias stable_id to a composite node.id are the bitbake/bibtex sites that
ARE WI-banod T1, and both analyzers build ids with their own f-strings that
never reach make_symbol_id, so no digest moves and v9's unqualified
compatibility claim stays true.

